---
description: Keeping RELEASE_NOTES.md up to date as features are built
globs:
alwaysApply: true
---
When you implement a change, update the `Unreleased` section of `RELEASE_NOTES.txt` if and only if the change is something a user would notice or care about.

Rules:
- Write from the user's perspective. Focus on what they can now do or what feels better — not what changed in the code.
- Keep entries short (one line). Plain English, no jargon.
- Do NOT add entries for: refactors, internal architecture changes, code cleanup, test changes, CI/CD changes, dependency bumps, bug fixes the user would never have noticed.
- DO add entries for: new features, visible UI changes, performance improvements the user would feel, fixed bugs that were affecting the user experience.
- Never add more than one bullet per logical change.
- Do not add a version number — entries go under `## Unreleased`.

Examples of good entries:
- Meditation timer now remembers your last used duration
- Fixed a crash when opening the app on first launch
- Stats page loads noticeably faster

Examples of bad entries (do not write these):
- Refactored HomeViewModel to use StateFlow
- Updated Riverpod to 2.5.1
- Moved API call to repository layer
